    [Performance][MoE] Fuse shared W8A8 MXFP activation quantization with an explicit path (#18474)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Shared experts using W8A8 MXFP run SwiGLU and activation MX quantization
    as separate kernels. Add an explicit
    `SharedExpertMLPPath.W8A8_MXFP_LINEAR` path that preserves both
    registered linears and fuses compatible activations with
    `npu_swiglu_group_quant(quant_mode=2)`.
    
    The enum selects the implementation family, not a guarantee of fusion.
    After Gate-Up, `_prepare_w8a8_mxfp_down_input` checks the actual tensor
    and Down scheme. Compatible inputs produce `PreparedLinearInput`; the
    existing Down linear consumes its FP8 values and E8M0 scales without
    quantizing again. Unsupported inputs use the original activation and
    scheme-owned preparation. Active LoRA retains the generic wrapper path.
    
    Fusion eligibility remains conservative: exact W8A8 MXFP scheme, plain
    SwiGLU, BF16 2D input, scale algorithm 0, 32-element groups, no TP input
    padding, parallel Down input, 128-element input-width alignment, and an
    available custom operator. Initialization consistency validation uses
    the same preparation helper.
    
    Both linear-wrapper paths share the existing scheduling:
    activation/preparation follows `shared_activation_overlap_start`, and
    Down remains after the event recorded before routed combine
    (`routed_combine_start`). Gate-Up submission, expert gating, other
    quantization paths, and the main-branch fix for prequantized activation
    inputs are preserved.
    
    For a `[36, 4096]` Gate-Up output, fusion removes one kernel launch and
    the write/read of a `[36, 2048]` BF16 intermediate (288 KiB of tensor
    traffic). End-to-end speedup requires NPU measurement.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    No new public configuration or API. Compatible W8A8 MXFP shared experts
    use fused activation quantization; unsupported configurations retain
    their original preparation behavior.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    - `python tests/ut/ops/test_shared_mxfp_activation.py -v`: all 14
    dependency-free logic tests passed. They load the production enum and
    methods, with mocked tensor metadata/device calls, and cover dispatch,
    Gate-Up wrapper preservation, LoRA, generic and other fused paths,
    fusion/fallback event order, expert gating, aligned/unsupported inputs,
    initialization consistency, missing-operator fallback, and Down
    consumption without requantization.
    - Updated existing CPU unit-test expectations for the explicit W8A8 MXFP
    path, including LoRA and incomplete weight initialization.
    - Local Ruff, formatting, Codespell with repository settings, whitespace
    checks, and mypy for the contract tests targeting Python 3.10/3.11/3.12
    passed.
    - [GitHub CI on
    4d5ee6559](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm-ascend/actions/runs/38433475205):
    full pre-commit (including repository hooks and mypy) passed; complete
    CPU unit-test jobs against vLLM main
    `8cc9aa5ad350f6f80cf3bebb298d410803ff75a9` and release `v0.30.0` both
    passed. DCO passed. The branch contains one linear commit so the CI
    rebase step also succeeds.
    - NPU numerical parity, graph replay, and full-model performance require
    Ascend hardware. The repository requires a maintainer's test-enabling
    label before selected NPU tests can run.
    
    - vLLM main:
    https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/commit/c4df37dfcc7f72ca41cd43801a468526ecf99a2b
    
